Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. APARTMENT - DAY

JAKE, 28, wiry, short brown hair, sits at the kitchen table with MA, 60s, tired eyes, brown hair. Sunlight cuts through the window onto the worn linoleum.

NARRATOR
Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. His face looked like a puzzle held together with glue. He wasn’t ugly, just had a few brawls over the years.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

NICK, 32, broad, reddish-brown hair, scars on chin and left eyebrow, walks in. A MAN on the curl bench drops his 40-pound bar and steps aside. Nick nods, loads a 50-pound bar.

NARRATOR
When he walked in the bar or the gym, people moved out of the way.

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND APARTMENT - DAY

Young JAKE, NICK, and MARK CONNER play kickball. Mark kicks the ball through a window. MRS. ENGEL storms out. Nick steps forward.

NICK
I did it.

NARRATOR
Nick took the blame. Ma paid for the window. A week later Nick gave her sixty bucks. He never told her it was Mark.

INT. APARTMENT - NIGHT

JAKE and NICK sit on the couch. Ma’s bedroom door is closed.

NARRATOR
We lived on the fifth floor of a brick building on Kirk Street in Milwaukee. Nick ran an assembly line at the metal factory. He had a 1980 Trans Am, burnt orange with yellow flames.

EXT. STREET - DAY

Nick’s Trans Am pulls up. People turn to look.

INT. APARTMENT - DAY

Nick stands in the kitchen, looking toward the bedroom.

NICK
What’s Ma doin’ back there at this time of day?

JAKE
I don’t know.

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn’t be sleeping all day.

JAKE
I’ll do better.

INT. PIZZERIA - DAY

JAKE walks toward the door. SIONNA, 26, short brown hair, pale skin, light blue eyes, trips and brushes into him. She wears a black V-neck and a fish necklace.

JAKE
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

JAKE
I work here. What’s your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

JAKE
Sionna? What does that mean?

SIONNA
My parents named me after an Irish goddess. The River Shannon.

JAKE
You want to come in for pizza?

SIONNA
I don’t eat pizza. But coffee sometime. You can call me.

NARRATOR
No girl had ever been interested in me. She looked back over her shoulder like she was trying to remember what I would look like tomorrow.

INT. APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma rummages through pill bottles on the counter, glassy-eyed.

MA
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

JAKE
Ma, we don’t go to church anymore.

Ma snaps back, confused.

MA
Oh, right. I see them.

NARRATOR
She was losing it. I would have to tell Nick.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

JAKE watches the Packers game. Nick enters, slaps his back.

NICK
Hey, little brother.

JAKE
Ma was confused today.

NICK
I knew she’d start going crazy cooped up. Take her to the store or something.

JAKE
I just needed to get away for a while.

Nick’s face hardens.

NICK
That’s more like it. Watch her tomorrow.

NARRATOR
Sometimes I really hated Nick inside. But I never told anybody.

EXT. STREET - NIGHT

JAKE sees the library across the block. He crosses.

INT. LIBRARY - NIGHT

JAKE approaches the desk.

JAKE
How do I take a book home?

LIBRARIAN
Do you have a library card?

JAKE
No.

She hands him a form. He writes his name and address. She gives him a temporary card. He walks the aisles, stops at classic literature, pulls Wuthering Heights.

NARRATOR
I ended up in the classic literature section. I picked Wuthering Heights.

INT. APARTMENT - NIGHT

JAKE reads under a lamp. Ma is asleep in the next room.

NARRATOR
It was about Heathcliff and Catherine. They really loved each other. It reminded me of Sionna.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

JAKE sits across from Sionna at a small table. Backpacks and textbooks surround them.

SIONNA
What are you reading?

JAKE
Wuthering Heights.

SIONNA
I read it. The love that never changes destroys him.

NARRATOR
Something inside me was coming awake. She made me feel smarter just by being around her.

JAKE
We should go to dinner Friday.

SIONNA
Absolutely.

EXT. STREET - DAY

JAKE walks away from the coffee shop, smiling.

NARRATOR
I knew one thing. There ain’t no way I could ever forgive my dad for leaving. Nick became my dad when I was eight.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

JAKE lies on a purple mat in shorts and a T-shirt, awkward in downward dog. Sionna is beside him. After class they stand.

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

JAKE
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
You caught on very quickly. You did like it, didn’t you?

JAKE
Yeah. I did.

NARRATOR
Afterward I felt calm. Making pizzas that night didn’t bother me. Nothing did.

INT. APARTMENT - DAY

Nick storms around the kitchen. Ma sits frozen in her chair. Jake enters.

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment yesterday. Where the hell were you?

JAKE
I was with someone. I met a girl.

Nick’s face reddens, fists clenched.

NICK
A girl. That’s worth all this. Try looking at your priorities, bro.

Nick slams the door on his way out.

NARRATOR
That’s all I need. Nick mad at me. I hardly ever get into it with Nick. I just don’t like arguing with people, especially Nick.

INT. LIBRARY - DAY

JAKE browses the exercise section, pulls a yoga book, then moves to world religion and selects a Buddhism title. He checks them out at the desk.

NARRATOR
I ended up in a section of the library with exercise books, and there in front of me were lots of books on yoga, so I picked one out. Next to this I came across a section on world religion. I had never thought too much about religion, but my mind drifted back to a couple conversations Sionna and I had about Buddhist thinking, so I checked out one of those too.

INT. APARTMENT - NIGHT

JAKE sits with the books open. He picks up the phone.

JAKE
I wished you would finally come over to my place, and even meet my Ma.

SIONNA (V.O.)
Are you sure? It’s kind of a big deal to meet your mother.

JAKE
Hell, she probably won’t even remember meeting you when she wakes up the next morning. She’s so forgetful now.

He flips through the Buddhism book.

NARRATOR
This part I read was about attachment, and how attaching ourselves to other people or things will make us miserable and cause suffering. The problem is that we are all looking for happiness, and the book says we look for happiness in the outside world, instead of realizing that the way we think about things can make us happy.

JAKE
(into phone)
What I really need to talk to you about is some stuff I read in a book about Buddhism.

SIONNA (V.O.)
Oh. I didn’t know you were interested in Buddhism.

JAKE
Well, remember when we were talking about meditating? I think I want to try it sometime.

INT. APARTMENT - DAY

MA watches a game show. JAKE enters from the kitchen.

JAKE
How’s it goin’ Ma?

MA
What? Oh, it’s you.

She looks confused. Her hand goes to her chest.

MA
It just feels like my heart is going to jump out of my chest! It’s so strange. I’m a little dizzy too.

JAKE grabs the phone.

INT. DOCTOR’S OFFICE - DAY

JAKE sits by the window staring at the grey sky. A NURSE calls his name. He follows her down the hall. DR. CARSON enters the exam room.

DR. CARSON
Hello, I’m Dr. Carson. I think I may have met you before. You’re the younger son, right?

JAKE
Yeah, that’s right. Uh, how’s she doin’ Doc?

DR. CARSON
Your mother’s blood pressure is dangerously high. It’s so high, I’m afraid of the risk of a stroke. My advice is that you take her directly to the emergency room.

JAKE
Whoa man. I can’t believe this. Are you sure it’s that bad?

DR. CARSON
Your mother has been confused, is that right? And she also experienced chest pressure this afternoon?

JAKE
Yeah, that’s right. She hasn’t been acting too normal lately.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

MA lies in bed. JAKE sits in the chair by the window. SIONNA enters with lunch. They walk outside.

NARRATOR
When I saw Sionna though, my heart did a little dance in my chest. Her eyes sparkled like diamonds. She made me feel excited and calm all at the same time.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

JAKE dozes in the chair. He startles awake, gasping.

NARRATOR
In the dream I was a soldier. Nick was running towards me. “Nick! Nick! Thank God you’re here! I thought I was going to die.” “That you are little brother” he hissed at me, and then suddenly stabbed the longest knife I have ever imagined into my chest. Nick had killed me.

JAKE stares at the sleeping MA, heart pounding.

INT. APARTMENT - NIGHT

JAKE shows MA the blue pill container.

JAKE
See Ma, right here is every day of the week, and every day will have the pills in you need. There’s even separations for morning and night.

MA
Oh my Lord, that looks a little overwhelming.

JAKE
No worries Ma. I’m goin’ to be here to make sure everything is okay.

MA
You’re such a good boy. You and Nick are like a God-send to me. What would I do without you two?

NICK enters, face red, boots heavy.

NICK
What’s up little Bro? You got Ma all tucked in? Man, you shoulda been a nurse!

JAKE
Knock it off man. Somebody has to make sure she stays on track. Where have you been?

NICK
Don’t start giving ME shit Bro. Where the hell do you think? I was at work.

JAKE
It’s eight o’clock at night Man. Ma’s been home for like four hours.

NICK
All right, look. I’m not giving you hell. Just what is the deal around here anyway? Are you going to keep her from strokin’ out, or what?

JAKE hands him the container.

NICK
Oh, holy shit! Aren’t you nurse Nancy! Well, well, how did you come up with this little idea anyway?

JAKE
If you must know, Sionna helped me figure it out.

NICK
If you must know, if you must know. Whatever. This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

JAKE
Stop it Nick! You haven’t even met her. Give her a fuckin’ break!

NICK stands, towering.

NICK
Don’t you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

JAKE
I’m not a bastard!

NICK
How the hell do you know? Maybe you are. You sure are a giant pussy – and that makes you and me way different Bro.

JAKE
You’re so Goddamn hateful. What the fuck got into you? Was Ma on crack or somethin’ when she was pregnant with you?

NICK slaps JAKE across the head. The door slams behind him. JAKE stands alone, ear ringing.

INT. APARTMENT - DAY

JAKE rubs his ear as he moves through the quiet rooms. He checks the clock. Two o’clock.

He prepares MA’s medication exactly as before and carries the container to her bedside.

MA lies still, eyes half-open.

JAKE sets the container down and adjusts her blanket. He sits in the chair beside the bed, the apartment silent around them.

INT. APARTMENT - DAY

JAKE sits motionless beside MA’s bed. His eyes drift to the window. The room fades.

EXT. SCHOOLYARD - DAY

Fourth-grade JAKE and BOBBY SWENSON fold over laughing, clutching their sides. JENNIFER BALES and SUE glare at them.

JENNIFER BALES
You guys are in so much trouble when Mrs. Stark finds out you’re making fun of her.

JAKE
You HAVE to learn some responsibility!

BOBBY SWENSON
No, YOU have to learn some responsibility!

They chant together, faces red. NICK approaches, hair wild.

NICK
What are you two numbskulls doin’?

JAKE
Oh, just the usual crap from Mrs. Stark. She thinks we need to learn some responsibility.

NICK slaps JAKE’s shoulder.

NICK
Little Bro, you better start listening, man. Ma is pissed at you already.

MRS. STARK strides up, glasses on her beaded necklace.

MRS. STARK
Mr. Olsen! Would you like to explain yourself to me before I send you in to talk to Mr. Pauls?

NICK steps forward.

NICK
He didn’t mean anything by it. He and Bobby were just goofing around. I’ll talk to him and Ma will talk to him. He’ll never act up in class again. I guarantee it.

MRS. STARK studies NICK, then softens.

MRS. STARK
Well, Nick, I know you’re a good student. I guess I’ll let it pass this time. YOU, young man, need to follow your brother’s example. And you, Bobby, your mother will be getting a note.

She pivots and marches away. JAKE turns to NICK.

JAKE
Nick, um thanks man. You saved my skin.

NICK smirks and walks off toward older boys.

INT. JAKE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

NICK musses JAKE’s hair.

NICK
I’ll always take care of you. Don’t forget that.

JAKE
I won’t Nick. I won’t.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Adult JAKE stares out the front window at green grass. He sighs, a faint grin on his face.

INT. APARTMENT - EVENING

NICK tosses his keys into the basket and kisses MA.

NICK
Those assholes next to me in the line almost caught me in the goddamn drill press today.

MA
Nick, do you have to swear?

NICK
Okay, okay, Ma. No, I won’t swear in front of ya.

NICK turns to JAKE.

NICK
So, what’s up with you?

JAKE
Not much. Just working and thinking about going to school.

NICK stares, eyes narrowing.

NICK
Going to school? How the hell are you going to pay for that? And what for?

JAKE
I’m going to the community college and getting a bachelor’s degree in business. Sionna told me I could qualify for a low-interest student loan.

NICK
Whoa, whoa, whoa dude. You have really done it now. I can’t believe you are letting this chick tell you what to do.

JAKE
This was my idea. It would really mean a lot to me if you could just step up and be on my side for once.

NICK’s voice rises.

NICK
Be on your side? Man, do you actually remember your own life? How many times have I saved your ass?

MA looks up, forehead creased, and turns back to the television.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

NICK, BOB, and SAMMY pass a pipe. Smoke curls. NICK laughs.

NICK
He’s got the perfect girlfriend. She has him wrapped right now.

JAKE leans back in the rocking chair.

JAKE
You three wouldn’t recognize class and intelligence if it jumped up and bit your ass.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Pool balls crack. Smoke hangs. NICK spots LINDSEY with a dark-haired man.

NICK
Who’s she with? Who’s that asshole?

JAKE
Nick, leave it alone.

NICK pushes past him. The dark-haired man steps forward.

DARK-HAIRED MAN
You got a problem with me?

NICK’s fists clench. JAKE plants himself in front of his brother.

JAKE
Nick, Nick, listen – you don’t have any problem with this guy. Leave it man, just leave it.

NICK
Keep out of it! It’s none of your Goddamn business. You’re going to be next if you don’t get out of my face asshole.

NICK’s right fist draws back. JAKE’s head snaps as the blow lands. JOE and the BOUNCER rush in. NICK head-butts the bouncer and breaks free. JAKE runs.

EXT. CITY STREETS - NIGHT

JAKE sprints, lungs burning, ears ringing. He reaches SIONNA’S door.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - NIGHT

SIONNA opens the door. JAKE collapses into her arms.

JAKE
Your brother tried to hit you.

SIONNA
No one does that. No one who really cares about you.

JAKE
He does care about me. I don’t know if we’ll ever even talk to each other again.

SIONNA strokes his face.

SIONNA
You have learned to use your brain. You can’t let him intimidate you.

JAKE
My purpose… what is my purpose?

SIONNA
We really won’t know until life is over.

JAKE laughs once, exhausted. SIONNA cups his jaw.

SIONNA
You’re exhausted. Let’s get some sleep. Why don’t you stay with me tonight?

JAKE rests his head in her lap. She massages his scalp.

JAKE
Sionna, the first time I met you, I felt so comfortable. It was like I had finally come home.

SIONNA
Me too honey. Me too.

Her eyes glisten.

SIONNA
By the way – the purpose of your life is to find the true self within you, and to honor it.

JAKE’s eyes close.

EXT. REEDY LAKE - NIGHT (DREAM)

The world is black and white. JAKE, tunic muddied, kneels at the water’s edge. Wind howls. Waves slap his boots. From the center of the lake a woman rises, long brown hair and light-blue eyes, white gown swirling though she remains dry. She lifts her arms like a conductor.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - NIGHT

JAKE bolts upright on the couch, nearly hitting SIONNA.

JAKE
What the?

SIONNA
Oh my gosh! What’s going on? We must have drifted off to sleep.

She rubs her eyes. JAKE checks the kitchen clock.

JAKE
Three a.m.

SIONNA
I had the craziest dream. You were an angel, and came out of the water. You protected us from Nick with some kind of magical force!

SIONNA giggles sleepily.

SIONNA
Well, that sounds about right. Let’s go to bed and get some sleep. We’ve had an interesting day.

JAKE
You can say that again.

They shuffle down the dark hallway.

INT. JAKE'S HOUSE - DAY

Months later. JAKE sits at the kitchen table with homework. MA dozes in her chair. SIONNA enters with a casserole dish.

SIONNA
Butternut squash, cheese, and crunchy nuts. Ma’s favorite now.

She helps JAKE fill pill containers.

SIONNA
Half the time you’re too tired to get it right.

JAKE
School, work, Ma… everything’s pulling at me.

SIONNA
You’re growing. I see it.

INT. CLASSROOM - DAY

JAKE listens to the TEACHER, a middle-aged man with a beer gut.

TEACHER
The science of thinking about thinking. Socrates said the unexamined life is not worth living.

JAKE stares at his Descartes notes.

INT. JAKE'S HOUSE - DAY

SIONNA flops on JAKE’s bed.

SIONNA
Yoga, store, notes on comparative religion, library, then here. Geez, what a day.

JAKE watches her mouth move.

JAKE
I love you.

SIONNA
Have you been listening to anything I said?

JAKE
Every word.

He kisses her. They roll on the bed laughing.

SIONNA
So what’s your paper about?

JAKE
Dualism and Descartes. Perception. Is the bed here when you don’t see it?

SIONNA
Does a tree that falls in the forest make a sound?

JAKE
Right. That kind of shit.

SIONNA
My parents were hippies. Mom got pregnant with me at Woodstock. Dad went by Mushroom, Mom by Jasmine. That’s how I got Sionna.

JAKE
Celtic goddess of the river Shannon. Possessor of wisdom.

SIONNA
You remembered.

JAKE
Where are they now?

SIONNA
Door County. Ten acres, chickens, a cow. Unpasteurized milk and all.

JAKE
Have you ever done drugs?

SIONNA
I probably smoked more secondhand pot before I was three than most adults do on purpose.

JAKE
Can you get some? We could hang out at your house and get really stoned.

SIONNA
I know lots of people at school. It’s not a big deal.

INT. PIZZA PLACE - NIGHT

JAKE works the counter. KEVIN MCCLEARY enters, arms like the Hulk, hair gelled.

KEVIN MCCLEARY
Haven’t seen you at the gym. Heard what happened at Joe’s. You stopped Nick from beating some guy’s ass. People say you clocked him. You’re the hottest topic in town.

JAKE
It wasn’t like that. Nick almost kicked my ass. Tell everybody it wasn’t like that.

KEVIN MCCLEARY
Sure, sure. But it’s all over town. Nick’s little brother is a badass.

JAKE
Don’t believe everything you hear.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - DAY

SIONNA and JAKE sit on the lumpy chocolate couch. She packs a blue ceramic pipe.

SIONNA
I’ve had this for ten or twelve years. I’ve been a little busy.

They smoke. Green Day plays.

SIONNA
Time grabs you by the wrist; directs you where to go.

JAKE
What do you think it means?

SIONNA
None of us knows what will happen, but we have to make the best of things. In the end everything is alright.

JAKE
I’m stoned.

SIONNA
I can sometimes see people’s auras. You’re a beautiful color of blue. Peaceful. Loyal.

JAKE
What color are you?

SIONNA
Blue or purplish. Faith. Mystical qualities.

JAKE
You have mystified me.

EXT. DAIRYLAND - DAY

JAKE and SIONNA eat soft-serve on cement benches. BOB approaches, flannel untucked over a beer belly.

BOB
Haven’t seen you since the big brawl at Joe’s. Your boyfriend single-handedly conquered the beast. Everybody’s bad-mouthing Nick. Saying he’s a pussy.

JAKE
Please stop those stupid rumors. Nick is still Nick.

BOB
Tell Nick I want to hang sometime.

JAKE
I haven’t seen Nick.

BOB
You mean you haven’t seen him either?

JAKE watches BOB walk away.

JAKE
Where is Nick?

INT. JAKE'S HOUSE - DAY

MA sits in her chair.

MA
I smell something burning.

JAKE finds the coffeepot boiled dry in the sink.

JAKE
Ma, how many times do I have to tell you NOT to try to cook anything?

MA starts to cry.

MA
I can’t remember anything anymore. I’m no good for anything. Nick doesn’t love me. He never comes to see me.

JAKE
I’ll go check on him. I’ll make sure everything is okay.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY

JAKE knocks. The door opens a slit, chain still attached. NICK’s crazy brown hair and pox-marked face appear.

NICK
What do YOU want?

JAKE
Will you let me in, please?

NICK
What for?

JAKE
It’s about time we talked this out. Ma is worried about you.

NICK
I really don’t need to talk to you man. Why don’t you just go home? Haven’t you done enough damage?

JAKE
Nick…please…let me in so we don’t have to talk through a crack.

NICK closes the door, unlocks the chain, opens it. The living room is a tornado of papers, trash, and clothes.

JAKE
Is everything okay?

NICK
What do YOU think, Moron? You’ve ruined my life! I can’t go anywhere. People are talkin’ shit about me. I can’t go to the gym. They’re sayin’ my little brother tried to kick my ass. All because of your big mouth!

JAKE
Nick, man, whoa. Slow down. I didn’t do anything.

NICK
You piece of shit. I supposed you started some rumor to make yourself look good. My reputation is dog shit. Thanks a lot Bro.

JAKE
Nick, I don’t know exactly what happened that night. It’s kind of a blur. I think I was just concerned you were going to hurt that guy for no reason. Anything I did just came out without thinking. I truly am sorry if you think I did something wrong. It’s not what I intended to do. I just wanted to hang out and enjoy the night. That’s all man, that’s all.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY

NICK holds up a finger aimed at JAKE.

NICK
Well, let me tell you something. You…YOU…are not the brother I knew, man. You’re not! All I know is now you’re some hot shit college kid with a hoity-toity girlfriend, and you know everything. Everything!

JAKE stands.

JAKE
Nick, I’m not like that. I’m not. You just need to come over and see Ma and me more often. If you met Sionna, I know you would like her. Yes, she is smart. Yes, I am trying to dig my way out of my stupid job. But, that’s all, man. That’s all there is to it. I don’t know everything. I hardly know anything. That’s the thing about going to school; you find out how much there is out there that you don’t know.

JAKE hangs his head and looks at his folded hands.

JAKE
That’s where I am. I’m working my ass off, and finding out how stupid I am. That’s it, man.

NICK stares. He sits back against the couch. The silence stretches. He looks around, sighs, then his eyes bore into JAKE.

NICK
I don’t really give a shit how hard you’re working. I don’t give two shits about your girlfriend, and I don’t give three shits about how dumb you are. But here is what I have to say. You are my little brother. I do care about you and Ma, and I will let it slide this time. But don’t you ever, EVER, interrupt me in public again. You got that?

NICK stands over JAKE.

NICK
I said, you got that?

JAKE
Yes, Nick. I got that. I will try to rectify the situation with your friends, which I want you to know, I have been doing since I heard the truly fucked up story that’s out there. I mean, really…do they think I could actually stop you? Really? That’s so dumb, I mean, it’s just a stupid made up story.

NICK
It is a stupid made up story. And yes, you will stop it. And yes, you could never stop me from doing what I want in a million years.

JAKE
So, will you see Ma? She’s dyin’ to see you, man.

NICK
Yeah, I’ll come over there tomorrow. Tell her I’ve been sick or somethin’. Don’t tell her about any of this shit. She doesn’t know about any of this shit, right?

JAKE
Of course not. You know Ma. Hell, she cried today because she forgot the burner was on in the kitchen. It took me a half hour to calm her down. She’s losing her memory big-time.

NICK
I know, I know. She’ll probably have to go to an old folks home one of these days. It’s the pits. I’m just tellin’ you man, that you have to make sure you’re takin’ care of her. She can’t be by herself, so you better be giving her her meds, or else she’s going to end up in the damn hospital.

JAKE
Absolutely.

JAKE stands and moves to the door.

JAKE
Thanks, Nick. It means a lot to me that you can forgive me. I’ll try to smooth things out. We’ll see you tomorrow then?

NICK
Yeah, yeah, see you tomorrow.

NICK opens the door. JAKE leaves. On the other side, rustling sounds continue.

INT. JAKE'S HOUSE - DAY

MA laughs with SIONNA.

MA
On Halloween, we would watch from the bushes, and my brothers would tip those outhouses over!

SIONNA smiles.

SIONNA
Sounds like you were a bunch of troublemakers.

MA
We were just plain hoodlums! But I didn’t have a care in the world. It really was a nice place to grow up. My parents were good people, hardworking, and regular members of the church.

MA sits still, then looks at JAKE.

MA
Why don’t we go to church anymore?

JAKE
You always say you don’t want to Ma. Do you want to? I’ll take you if you want to.

MA
Yes, I do. That would be fine. Let’s go next Sunday. It’s been too long.

JAKE
Okay Ma, we’ll go.

A knock at the door. JAKE opens it. NICK walks in.

MA
Oh My God! It’s you Nick! I thought we would never see you again. Where have you been hiding?

NICK leans in and kisses MA on the cheek.

NICK
Oh, you know, just busy working and crap like that. How ya doin’ Ma? This one been treatin’ you all right?

MA
Silly you, of course, of course. We are all just fine.

SIONNA enters with two plates of pie. She wipes her hands and holds one out.

SIONNA
I’m Sionna. You must be Nick. Nice to meet you, I’ve heard so much about you.

NICK reluctantly shakes her hand.

NICK
Uh, yea, likewise.

SIONNA
You’re just in time for pie.

NICK
No thanks. I can’t eat that crap. Sugar makes me feel bloated and sick.

SIONNA
Alright, that’s fine. Let me know if you want anything. Don’t want to make you feel left out.

NICK
Right.

Silence, only the sound of forks on plates.

JAKE
This is really good Nick…are you sure you don’t want some?

NICK
No, man. I’m sure.

NICK stares Sionna up and down.

NICK
So, I hear you’re a university lady.

SIONNA
That’s right.

NICK
What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
Well, that’s a tough one. I have a major in English, and will have a minor in Philosophy, so, I am thinking college professor, if I can get my student teaching done and write a decent thesis. It will be another year of work before I can honestly call myself a graduate, however.

NICK nods slowly.

NICK
Well, professor, I hope the best for ya. It’s tough out there, and, like I was tellin’ my kid brother here….sometimes all that school just doesn’t pay off. So, I hope you can break through that glass ceiling, or whatever. Because, you know, it’s even harder for a woman to teach at that level, or so I’ve heard.

SIONNA sets her plate down.

SIONNA
Well, Nick, I have to disagree with you there. As a matter of fact, in the last forty years, bachelor’s degrees earned by female students have risen from about 35 to 55 percent. Actually, there are more females in college today than males. We make up about sixty percent of all undergraduate students. This is very encouraging, and impressive, if you consider many of those women are schooling part-time, juggling the demands of family and work. In my line of thinking, I just don’t see how the future of education cannot be significantly changed for the better, by the presence of more female professors. You can’t keep a good woman down, so to speak.

SIONNA picks up her plate and takes MA’s plate into the kitchen.

MA
Oh my.

NICK
Well, if you ask me, that might be one of the reasons this country is heading down the flippin’ tubes. The women in this country can’t stay home and take care of their kids.

NICK glances at MA.

NICK
No disrespect Ma, I know you had no choice.

SIONNA returns.

SIONNA
You might be right in saying that children need the guidance of a mother figure. And you also may be right in saying that not only your mother, but many other women today, have no choice in the matter. Once you figure in the need for a dual income family just to make ends meet, and the fact that single parent families are climbing at a substantial rate, this is true. Women cannot afford to stay home.

INT. JAKE'S HOUSE - DAY

JAKE enters, sets down his keys. The apartment is quiet. He checks the answering machine. The red light blinks.

JAKE
Five missed calls.

He hits play. OFFICER MIKE DUPUIS's voice fills the room.

OFFICER MIKE DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olson, this is Officer Mike Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff's Department. We need to talk to you regarding your family.

NURSE PATRICIA OWENS (V.O.)
Hello Mr. Olson, this is Patricia Owens, one of the nurses at Frodo Hospital, with an update on your family members.

JAKE stares at the machine, then grabs the phone and dials.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

JAKE sits across from OFFICER MIKE DUPUIS, a big man with graying hair and a thin mustache. JAKE's hands shake around a Styrofoam cup of water.

OFFICER MIKE DUPUIS
Last night about 8 p.m. they were hit broadside at Werner and Lowe. Your brother is in serious condition at Frodo. Your mother passed away at the scene.

JAKE sets the cup down. Water spills.

JAKE
Nick was driving?

OFFICER MIKE DUPUIS
Toxicology is pending. We think he may have been drinking.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

JAKE stands in the doorway. NICK lies in bed, right leg in traction, chest tube in place, face bruised yellow and green. Wires and machines surround him. NICK's erratic red hair sticks up above the mattress.

NICK opens his eyes.

NICK
Hey, bro.

JAKE stays in the doorway.

NICK
I had three Jack and Cokes. I can't remember what hit us.

JAKE
They took care of Ma. She's at St. Mary's.

NICK
The cops say vehicular manslaughter. I didn't mean to hurt her.

JAKE
Whose fault was it, Nick?

JAKE turns and walks out.

INT. SACRED HEART FUNERAL HOME - DAY

JAKE and SIONNA stand with FATHER JOSEPH beside a moderate casket. SIONNA points to lining options. JAKE nods without looking.

SIONNA
This one.

FATHER JOSEPH
The plot is ready at the top of the hill.

INT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

A handful of mourners. BOB stands solemn. JAKE throws a handful of dirt onto the maple lid. It hits with a muted thunk.

INT. JAKE'S HOUSE - NIGHT

JAKE sits on the edge of the bed. SIONNA sleeps. He stares at the wall.

JAKE (V.O.)
I lost my mom. And it seemed I had also lost my brother.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

JAKE works the ovens. BOB enters.

BOB
Man, what is up? Nick barely survived. Cops might lock him up.

JAKE
I'm not okay with Nick. I'm not okay with Ma being dead.

BOB backs toward the door.

BOB
I'm sorry, man. If you need anything...

JAKE stares into the oven door as the phone rings.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY

NICK sits on the edge of a worn couch, leg propped on a crate. He lights a Marlboro, ignores the no-smoking sign on the wall, and exhales toward the ceiling. A stack of unpaid bills and a phone book lie open on the table.

NICK (V.O.)
That little bitch. It's all that little bitch's fault.

He stands, limps to the window, stares out at the gray street.

NICK (V.O.)
I had lots of time to think in that hospital. The cops say I was drunk driving. What about my pain and suffering?

EXT. BUS STOP - DAY

Cold wind. NICK waits among a handful of strangers, hands shoved in his pockets. The bus pulls up. He boards.

INT. CITY BUS - DAY

NICK rides in the back. Rain streaks the windows. He watches the passing buildings.

NICK (V.O.)
Bob's the only one who came through. The rest of them can go to hell. She messed everything up. School, big ideas, all of it.

EXT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - DAY

NICK steps off the bus two blocks away. He walks the cracked sidewalk, past dead plants in pots, and stops at the duplex driveway. Jake's bike is gone. He knocks hard on the door.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - DAY

SIONNA opens the door a crack, then wider when she sees him.

SIONNA
Nick? Is that you?

NICK steps inside without waiting. A gray cat slips past his legs. SIONNA closes the door.

NICK
Yeah, it's me. Can I talk to you?

SIONNA
Definitely. Have a seat.

NICK
No, that won't be necessary. I'm guessing you're alone.

SIONNA
Um, yeah. Is that what you wanted?

NICK
Nope. I want to talk to you. I don't suppose you have any idea what the fuck I've been going through lately, do you?

SIONNA sits on the edge of the brown chair, still holding a dish rag.

SIONNA
Look, Nick, whatever is on your mind, I'm sure we can work through it. Would you like some coffee?

NICK
No, I don't want some fucking coffee. You're the reason I'm probably going to jail. You know that?

He moves closer. SIONNA stands slowly.

SIONNA
Let's talk about this rationally. What makes you think I'm responsible for you going to jail?

NICK
If it wasn't for you messing around with my brother, everything would be cool. He would be taking care of Ma. She would be alive right now and I would be working. How about paying for my hospital and lawyer bills? You got twenty grand? How about serving time in jail for the stupid accident? This is all your fault.

He grabs her neck with his left hand and pushes her against the wall. She makes small, panicked sounds. With his right hand he pulls the switchblade from his coat, flicks it open in front of her face.

NICK
Break it off with him tonight or else. Then you're going to get your hands on some cash and get ready to hand it over. I'll be back in one week and I better see some fucking cash or this blade goes into your little neck. If you so much as leak one word to the cops or anyone else, remember that I have lots of friends. They don't give a shit if you're alive or dead.

The front door slams open. JAKE rushes in.

JAKE
Holy shit, Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

JAKE grabs for NICK's arm. They struggle. The knife drives into SIONNA's chest. Blood sprays. She makes a wet, choking sound and slides down the wall. NICK elbows JAKE in the head, wipes the blade on her shirt, and shoves past his brother.

NICK
I got to get out of here.

He runs out the door into the rain.

EXT. SEATTLE WATERFRONT - DAY

JAKE stands on the ferry deck, coffee in one hand, burrito in the other. The gray water stretches out. He walks inside, takes a seat at a table. A small boy runs past. JAKE watches him.

JAKE (V.O.)
The child is violently thrust over the banister. I jump with fear and throw my left hand over the side...

The boy is suddenly at the railing, tipping. JAKE lunges, grabs the boy's ankle with finger and thumb. The boy hangs limp, upside down. JAKE's waist cuts into the banister. He cannot use his right hand. Sweat beads on his forehead.

The image dissolves. JAKE jolts awake in his seat, gasping.

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - DAY

Rain mist falls. JAKE walks, hood up. He passes a man playing keyboard in a gray felt hat. JAKE continues toward Pike's Place Market.

JAKE (V.O.)
Seven years since my life suddenly stopped being what it was. Seven years of loneliness. Seven years of hatred for my brother.

INT. LOWELL'S RESTAURANT - DAY

JAKE sits at a window table, third coffee in front of him. Mountains are lost in low clouds. A cruise ship sits docked. The waitress brings a cinnamon roll. JAKE stares at it, lost.

JAKE (V.O.)
I found myself suddenly awake again, back up against the wall, trying to support her body weight...

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - NIGHT (FLASHBACK)

JAKE lowers SIONNA to the floor, blood on his hands. He rips her flannel shirt away, stacks his hands over the wound near her left shoulder. Sirens approach.

JAKE
Hurry up. Hurry up.

Paramedics burst in, take over. A cop pulls JAKE aside.

COP
Mr. Olson, you say your brother stabbed your girlfriend?

JAKE
Her name is Sionna McAllister. Nick Olson is my brother. He stabbed her.

INT. LOWELL'S RESTAURANT - DAY (BACK TO PRESENT)

JAKE stares at the cinnamon roll, frosting dripping.

JAKE (V.O.)
She finally blamed me for everything. We sat in the front room. She said she couldn't feel herself anymore.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - DAY (FLASHBACK)

SIONNA sits in the rocking chair, blank stare. JAKE squats in front of her.

JAKE
Please know that I love you. You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
As long as there is any doubt, I can't go on with you. Maybe we just need a break.

She stands and walks to the bedroom. JAKE hangs his head, fingers tearing at his hair.

INT. LOWELL'S RESTAURANT - DAY (BACK TO PRESENT)

JAKE pushes the plate away.

JAKE (V.O.)
I had to find forgiveness.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles flicker. JAKE and ten others sit in the dark room. The instructor speaks softly about gratefulness. JAKE's eyes close.

INT. JAKE'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

JAKE lies on the couch beside his orange-and-white cat, BOZO. He drifts into a dream.

EXT. MAGICAL HILL ROAD - DAY (DREAM)

JAKE walks a winding dirt road through bright green hills. An Indian sage in white wrap walks beside him.

SAGE
Stop judging things. All things are an illusion. You create your own reality.

The sage vanishes. JAKE wakes smiling, grabs a notebook, writes.

INT. JAKE'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

JAKE closes the notebook.

JAKE (V.O.)
I had to see Nick. I had to put this behind me forever.

EXT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE - DAY

JAKE parks the rental car, walks across gravel to the entrance. Metal detectors. Guards. He signs in.

INT. PRISON VISITING ROOM - DAY

JAKE sits at the plexiglass. NICK is led in, older, graying, thinner. They pick up phones.

JAKE
I'm here to tell you that even though you are my brother, I am no longer interested in seeing you or hearing anything you have to say. You're an asshole that killed my mother. You're an asshole that held me down my entire life. You're an asshole for trying to kill the love of my life. But I forgive you. I forgive you because it sets me free.

NICK stares, tears falling.

NICK
I'm so sorry. You're so right. I did do all that shit. You're a big man, and I'm a small one.

GUARD
Time's up.

NICK
I love you.

JAKE
I still love you for being my brother.

NICK is led away. JAKE hangs up the phone, sits a moment, then stands.

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - DAY

JAKE walks, lighter. He smiles to himself.

JAKE (V.O.)
That part of my life was closed now. I was free to live without fear, guilt, sadness, or judgment.

He passes the barista, CHRIS, at her coffee shop window. She waves. He waves back, keeps walking.

FADE OUT

THE END
